<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ali ibn Daud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">17th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ali ibn Daud was the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> who in 1647 founded an independent dynasty in the walled city, after the leadership of the Adal state had moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aussa">Aussa</a>. His descendants ruled Harar and its farmlands, surrounded by Oromo-settled countryside, until the Egyptian occupation of 1875. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> minted coins and taxed the caravan trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, khat and other goods.</p></div>
